{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>All, etc.</b> Now he mentioned all the nations — these are human beings — and afterward the plants and the animals. The sense: after all the nations are as nothing," "And the mem of <b>מאפס</b> is like the mem of המה מהבל יחד (Psalms 62:10), as though he said: they are reckoned less than nothing. And the wonder from the Gaon [Saadia], of blessed memory, who said that human beings are more honored than the angels of HaShem — but did he not call this passage?"], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 40, :notes {1 "The final clause 'but did he not call this passage' (והלא קרא זאת הפרשה) is elliptical; translated hyper-literally as the syntax is obscure and may be truncated or corrupt."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:57:10.003810530Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}